# Isolating, which it can only be because there is no index. Each plan is written from one type and
# named after it, with that type as its single originating element, so Gradle knows which source
# file each generated plan belongs to and can regenerate or delete exactly that one.
#
# This was AGGREGATING while the processor wrote a META-INF/services file listing every plan. Filer
# cannot append to a resource, so that file had to be rewritten whole on every build — and on an
# incremental build the processor sees only the sources that changed. It would rewrite the index
# with a fraction of the plans in it and silently put the rest back on the reflective path, which
# is worst for a wrapper type nobody edits and everything masks. GeneratedMaskPlanCompiler asks for
# a plan by name instead, and nothing is shared between two types any more.
ch.raph.datamask.processor.plan.MaskPlanProcessor,ISOLATING
